1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Module 2.

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:05,870
In Module 1 you built a
solid Python foundation.

3
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:11,552
Now we meet the engine behind every
agent in this course: the large language model.

4
00:00:11,772 --> 00:00:17,092
Before writing a single API call, you
need a clear mental model of what these

5
00:00:17,092 --> 00:00:20,961
systems do, and just as
importantly, what they don't do.

6
00:00:22,261 --> 00:00:25,994
You will be able to explain
next-token prediction in plain words.

7
00:00:26,344 --> 00:00:30,961
Understand tokens, and why they decide
your cost, your limits, and your speed.

8
00:00:31,311 --> 00:00:34,611
Reason about the context
window, and what fills it up.

9
00:00:34,961 --> 00:00:39,865
And know which controls you actually
have over generation, on current models.

10
00:00:41,165 --> 00:00:44,351
Here is the core idea,
simplified but accurate.

11
00:00:44,701 --> 00:00:47,752
A language model takes
the text so far as input.

12
00:00:47,972 --> 00:00:53,567
During training it saw an enormous
amount of text, and its weights encode patterns

13
00:00:53,567 --> 00:00:55,069
of how text continues.

14
00:00:55,419 --> 00:00:57,502
Its output is not a whole answer.

15
00:00:57,722 --> 00:01:00,800
It is a probability for
every possible next token.

16
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:04,012
These percentages
are just an illustration.

17
00:01:04,362 --> 00:01:09,386
One token is chosen and appended to the
text, and the whole thing goes back in to

18
00:01:09,386 --> 00:01:10,688
predict the next one.

19
00:01:10,908 --> 00:01:15,393
A long answer is just this loop,
run hundreds or thousands of times.

20
00:01:15,743 --> 00:01:17,830
This explains a lot of behaviour.

21
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:21,713
The model produces
plausible text, not looked-up facts.

22
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:27,298
It can be confidently wrong, which is
often called hallucination, and its knowledge

23
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:29,237
stops at its training cut-off.

24
00:01:29,457 --> 00:01:34,649
That's why agents give models tools and
documents to check facts, which is exactly

25
00:01:34,649 --> 00:01:36,169
what this course builds.

26
00:01:37,469 --> 00:01:38,812
So what is a token?

27
00:01:39,032 --> 00:01:44,626
It's a chunk of text, often a whole
short word, a piece of a longer word, a space,

28
00:01:44,626 --> 00:01:46,127
or a punctuation mark.

29
00:01:46,477 --> 00:01:51,740
For example, agents call tools, full
stop, might be split into four tokens.

30
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:53,532
This is an illustration.

31
00:01:53,752 --> 00:01:57,211
The exact split depends
on each model's tokenizer.

32
00:01:57,561 --> 00:02:01,930
A rough rule of thumb for English
is about four characters per token.

33
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:07,734
Code, unusual words, and many other
languages use more tokens for the same text.

34
00:02:08,084 --> 00:02:12,793
When you need an exact number, don't
guess, and don't use another company's

35
00:02:12,793 --> 00:02:13,421
tokenizer.

36
00:02:13,641 --> 00:02:19,233
Ask the API: it has a token counting
endpoint for the exact model you're using.

37
00:02:19,453 --> 00:02:21,233
You'll use it in lesson five.

38
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:24,591
Tokens matter for three reasons.

39
00:02:24,811 --> 00:02:25,772
First, cost.

40
00:02:25,992 --> 00:02:31,862
You pay per input token and per output
token, and output tokens usually cost several

41
00:02:31,862 --> 00:02:32,631
times more.

42
00:02:32,981 --> 00:02:34,037
Second, limits.

43
00:02:34,257 --> 00:02:38,105
Every request has a
maximum size, measured in tokens.

44
00:02:38,455 --> 00:02:39,606
And third, speed.

45
00:02:39,826 --> 00:02:45,118
Replies are generated one token at
a time, so long answers take longer.

46
00:02:45,338 --> 00:02:48,912
Streaming, in lesson four,
makes that wait feel much shorter.

47
00:02:50,212 --> 00:02:55,739
The context window is the maximum
number of tokens the model can consider in one

48
00:02:55,739 --> 00:02:58,158
request, input and output together.

49
00:02:58,508 --> 00:03:04,038
Everything you send goes in: the system
prompt with your instructions, the whole

50
00:03:04,038 --> 00:03:09,016
conversation so far, any documents
or tool results, and the new message.

51
00:03:09,366 --> 00:03:12,153
Current models have very large windows.

52
00:03:12,373 --> 00:03:19,092
Claude Opus 5.5, the model we use in
this course, accepts up to one million tokens.

53
00:03:19,442 --> 00:03:24,865
The reply must fit too, and it has its
own cap: up to one hundred and twenty-eight

54
00:03:24,865 --> 00:03:27,313
thousand output tokens on this model.

55
00:03:27,663 --> 00:03:30,337
A huge window does not
mean you should fill it.

56
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:36,281
Every token you send is billed on every
request, and focused context usually gives

57
00:03:36,281 --> 00:03:37,328
better answers.

58
00:03:37,548 --> 00:03:42,194
Managing what goes into the
window is a core agent engineering skill.

59
00:03:43,494 --> 00:03:44,986
One more crucial fact.

60
00:03:45,206 --> 00:03:49,395
When you chat with an assistant, it
seems to remember what you said earlier.

61
00:03:49,745 --> 00:03:53,160
But the model itself has
no memory between requests.

62
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:57,110
Your application sends the
whole conversation every time.

63
00:03:57,330 --> 00:03:59,674
The model's weights
don't change while you chat.

64
00:03:59,894 --> 00:04:03,973
If your code forgets to send the
history, the conversation is gone.

65
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:06,193
You'll see this happen in lesson three.

66
00:04:07,493 --> 00:04:10,086
You control generation with a few levers.

67
00:04:10,306 --> 00:04:13,447
The system prompt sets
the role, rules, and style.

68
00:04:13,797 --> 00:04:16,223
Max tokens caps the length of the reply.

69
00:04:16,573 --> 00:04:20,963
Effort controls how much
reasoning the model does, from low to max.

70
00:04:21,183 --> 00:04:25,905
Higher effort means better
answers on hard problems, but more tokens.

71
00:04:26,255 --> 00:04:32,185
And you choose the model itself: Opus,
Sonnet, or Haiku, trading capability against

72
00:04:32,185 --> 00:04:33,257
cost and speed.

73
00:04:33,607 --> 00:04:37,770
You may have read about
temperature and top p in older tutorials.

74
00:04:37,990 --> 00:04:42,496
On current Claude models those
sampling settings are no longer accepted.

75
00:04:42,716 --> 00:04:46,688
You steer the model with
instructions, effort, and the model you pick.

76
00:04:47,988 --> 00:04:48,795
Quick check.

77
00:04:49,015 --> 00:04:52,493
Your app sends a new request
without the earlier messages.

78
00:04:52,713 --> 00:04:53,657
What happens?

79
00:04:59,357 --> 00:05:01,049
The model simply doesn't know.

80
00:05:01,269 --> 00:05:03,418
It has no memory between requests.

81
00:05:03,638 --> 00:05:06,193
It only sees what
you send in that request.

82
00:05:07,493 --> 00:05:11,070
A language model predicts
the next token, over and over.

83
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:16,913
It produces plausible text, not
looked-up facts, so important claims need checking.

84
00:05:17,263 --> 00:05:22,564
Tokens drive cost, limits, and
speed, and the API can count them exactly.

85
00:05:22,914 --> 00:05:28,090
Everything must fit in the context
window, and your app resends the history every

86
00:05:28,090 --> 00:05:28,410
time.

87
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:34,569
Next lesson, you make your first real API
call, and take the response apart piece by

88
00:05:34,569 --> 00:05:34,984
piece.
